🔥 Alternative Quick Fix (without changing docker-compose.yml)
If you don't want to modify docker-compose.yml, you can restart Django inside the container on port 8000:



docker exec -it django_container bash python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000
Now your existing docker-compose.yml (mapping 8200:8000) will work.

🔹 Try opening: http://localhost:8200/


## Test API in Postman

![alt text](image.png)

http://localhost:8200/api/

✅ Step 1: Use Token-Based Authentication (If Applicable)
If your API is using JWT (JSON Web Token) authentication, follow these steps:

First, obtain a token by sending a POST request to your login endpoint.


POST http://localhost:8200/api/token/
In the Body (form-data or JSON), include:


{
  "username": "your_username",
  "password": "your_password"
}
This should return an access token.

✅ Step 1: Copy the Access Token
From the response body, copy the value of "access" (the JWT access token).
✅ Step 2: Use the Token in Your API Request
Open Postman and go to your GET or POST request (e.g., http://localhost:8200/api/users/).
Navigate to the Authorization tab.
Select Bearer Token as the Auth Type.
Paste the access token into the token field.
Click Send to retry the request.